// normalize.comp — AVIAN-MORPH Vulkan compute shader (L2 normalization).
//
// Mirrors the C11 kernel avian_normalize_f32 in src/c11/avian_norm.c:
//   out[i] = in[i] / norm,  norm = sqrt(sum of in[i]^2),
//   for i in [0, n). The input is N f32 values. The output is
//   N f32 values. The caller passes n, the element count.
//
// Design decision (honest, documented): this shader performs the
// DIVISION step only. The norm comes from a push constant, computed
// by the host. The host computes the norm with the same fmaf
// accumulation order as the C kernel (left to right from +0.0f,
// then sqrtf) and passes the validated norm in. Rationale:
//   - The norm is a reduction over the whole vector. A
//     single-workgroup shared-memory reduction caps the vector at
//     the workgroup size unless strided, and a strided tree
//     reduction accumulates in a different order than the C
//     kernel's strict left-to-right fmaf chain, so the shader's
//     norm would differ from the C kernel's norm by rounding.
//   - The host already computes the sum of squares to validate
//     the preconditions (the norm must be finite and nonzero
//     before dispatch), so passing that validated norm in is
//     exact and keeps the shader's division identical to the C
//     kernel's division step.
//   - The shader therefore does NOT recompute the norm. A
//     dispatch with an unvalidated norm (zero, infinite, NaN,
//     or computed in a different accumulation order) is a host
//     error, not a shader error.
//
// Contract (same as the C kernel; see src/c11/CONTRACT_norm.md):
//   - The host validates before dispatch, using the same checks as
//     the C kernel: buffers are non-NULL and non-overlapping,
//     output and input each hold at least n values, every input
//     element is finite, the sum of squares is finite, and the
//     norm is finite and nonzero. The shader performs the
//     arithmetic only; it does not re-validate.
//   - Each invocation computes exactly one output element: one
//     correctly-rounded f32 division, out[i] = in[i] / norm.
//     There is no cross-element reduction in the shader.
//     Deterministic: the same inputs give the same outputs.
//   - The division cannot overflow: every |in[i]| is at most the
//     norm, so every |out[i]| is at most 1.
//   - A zero input component divided by the (positive) norm keeps
//     its sign (+0.0f / norm is +0.0f, -0.0f / norm is -0.0f),
//     the same IEEE division sign rule as the C kernel.
//   - n == 0 is valid. The host dispatches zero workgroups and
//     writes nothing.
//
// Binding layout (set 0):
//   binding 0 — readonly input buffer (float data[], N values)
//   binding 1 — output buffer (float data[], N values)
//   push constant — norm (float, host-computed, validated),
//                   n (uint, element count)
//
// This shader is compiled to SPIR-V and validated with spirv-val.
// It is NOT executed here: there is no GPU on this machine, so the
// CPU-vs-GPU conformance comparison is UNVERIFIED (see
// CONTRACT_norm.md).

#version 450

layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) readonly buffer InputBuffer {
    float data[];
} input_buf;

layout(set = 0, binding = 1) buffer OutputBuffer {
    float data[];
} output_buf;

layout(push_constant) uniform PushConstants {
    float norm;
    uint  n;
} pc;

void main() {
    uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    if (i >= pc.n) {
        return;
    }
    output_buf.data[i] = input_buf.data[i] / pc.norm;
}
